Package: ecem
Title: Elicited Priors for Coarsening Regimes in Coarsened Exact Matching
Version: 0.0.0.9000
Authors@R: c(
    person("Axel", "Dery", role = c("aut", "cre"), email = "you@example.com"),
    person("Chris", "Alcantara", role = "aut"),
    person("David A.", "Armstrong II", role = "aut"),
    person("Laura", "Stephenson", role = "aut")
    )
Description: Implements the multiple-draw coarsened exact matching (CEM)
    workflow developed in "Elicited Priors for Coarsening Regimes in
    Coarsened Exact Matching." Coarsening cutpoints for each covariate can
    be elicited (uncertain, drawn from a range), fixed, exact-matched,
    excluded, or specified as a set of competing regimes (e.g. lifecycle
    vs. generational theories of where a covariate should be coarsened).
    Repeated draw-match-estimate cycles are pooled via Rubin's rules, with
    diagnostics for whether that pooling is valid: a pre-matching flatness
    screen, and a congeniality test run on a matched, common-support-
    restricted, CEM-weighted fixed specification, using a
    heteroskedasticity-robust Wald test rather than the earlier
    resampling-based design this supersedes (see the accompanying paper's
    appendix). When pooling is not valid, a Simonsohn-style existence
    test is provided as a fallback. When the number of achievable
    coarsenings is small enough, exact enumeration replaces Monte Carlo
    draws outright, eliminating simulation error. Covariance-based
    diagnostics let the analyst label the pooled estimate as an FSATT,
    ATT, or ATE.
